Sunlight darkens pigmentation and melasma and slows the fading of acne marks. A sunscreen used every day, in the right amount, is part of treating these problems, not an extra.

## Choosing one

- Broad spectrum (protects against UVA and UVB), SPF 30 or more.
- A gel or fluid texture is easier to wear in humid weather than a thick cream.
- If you have acne-prone skin, look for a label that says non-comedogenic.

## How much and when

Most people apply far less than the amount the SPF is tested at. For the face and neck, a common guide is two finger-lengths of sunscreen. Apply it 15 minutes before going out.

Reapply every two to three hours when you are outdoors, and after sweating heavily or washing your face. Indoors near a window, one morning application is usually enough.

